Qore openldap module

INTRODUCTION
------------
The Qore openldap module provides LDAP functionality to Qore by using the
openldap (http://www.openldap.org) client library to provide a Qore API to
talk to LDAP servers.

The LDAP API is standard but this module was specifically designed to compile
and link with the openldap APIs and has not been tested with other LDAP APIs.

The module includes SASL bind support, including GSSAPI-relevant hardening
options such as channel binding, security strength factors, maximum security
layer buffer size, and host canonicalization control.

see docs for more information


LICENSE
-------
This module is released under a choice of two licenses: LGPL 2.1 and MIT, see
COPYING.LGPL and COPYING.MIT for more information


BUILDING
--------
Requires Qore 2.3+, OpenLDAP headers and libraries, and Cyrus SASL development
headers to build. Debian packages use the matching Qore 3 SDK.

CMake Build (Recommended)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
mkdir build && cd build
cmake .. -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=release
make
make install

If the qore library cannot be found, you can specify QORE_DIR:
cmake .. -DQORE_DIR=<dir>

If the openldap library or headers cannot be found, you can specify OpenLDAP_DIR:
cmake .. -DOpenLDAP_DIR=<dir>


Autotools Build (Legacy)
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
To configure the build, execute:
        ./configure --disable-debug

If the qore library cannot be found, then you can use:
        ./configure --disable-debug --with-qore=<dir>

If the openldap library or headers cannot be found, then you can use:
        ./configure --disable-debug --with-openldap=<dir>

(or set the OPENLDAP_DIR environment variable before calling configure)

The qore binary also needs to be in the path so configure can determine the module directory

Then execute:
        make && make install

(or 'make && sudo make install' as needed)


QUESTIONS
---------
Please direct any questions to:
david@qore.org

Strict documentation builds
---------------------------
Use -DQORE_OPENLDAP_STRICT_DOCS=ON when configuring CMake to reject reference
warnings. Native documentation reads the generated public API and guides;
private implementation helpers are not part of the published reference.
